#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# trig_reduce

#% text
# `trig_reduce(e)` writes products and powers of sines and cosines as sums of sines and cosines of multiple angles.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The product-to-sum formulas are applied until no product of trigonometric functions remains; the result is linear in sines and cosines.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
trig_reduce(sin(x)^2)
#% output
#= 1/2 - (1/2)*cos(2*x)

#% cell
trig_reduce(sin(x)*cos(y))
#% output
#= (1/2)*sin(x + y) + (1/2)*sin(x - y)

#% cell
trig_reduce(cos(x)^3)
#% output
#= (1/4)*cos(3*x) + (3/4)*cos(x)

#% cell
trig_reduce(sin(x)^2*cos(x)^2)
#% output
#= 1/8 - (1/8)*cos(4*x)

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# An integral is easier in the reduced form:

#% cell
integrate(trig_reduce(sin(x)^4), x)
#% output
#= (-1/4)*sin(2*x) + (1/32)*sin(4*x) + (3/8)*x

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `trig_expand` goes the other way; the result equals `cos(x)^3` once `sin(x)^2` is replaced by `1 - cos(x)^2`:

#% cell
trig_expand(trig_reduce(cos(x)^3))
#% output
#= (1/4)*cos(x)^3 + (3/4)*cos(x) - (3/4)*cos(x)*sin(x)^2

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `trig_expand`, `trig_to_exp`, `simplify`
